# ZYRIONOS — FRONTEND INTERACTION & STATE ARCHITECTURE
## DOCUMENT 06
### Enterprise Interaction, State, Feedback & UI Behaviour Specification

Document Status: FINAL FOUNDATION
Version: 1.0
Scope: Complete ZyrionOS Frontend
Audience: Frontend Engineers, Backend Engineers, AI Coding Agents, QA, Product, Design
Authority: This document defines frontend behaviour and interaction rules.

======================================================================
1. PURPOSE
======================================================================

ZyrionOS frontend must never behave like a collection of independent pages.

Every interaction must follow a predictable enterprise interaction model.

This document defines:

- Click behaviour
- Navigation behaviour
- Page transitions
- Loading states
- Empty states
- Success states
- Error states
- Disabled states
- Processing states
- Confirmation flows
- Modal behaviour
- Drawer behaviour
- Form behaviour
- API request behaviour
- Optimistic updates
- Refresh behaviour
- Unsaved-change behaviour
- Search behaviour
- Filtering behaviour
- Pagination behaviour
- Workspace behaviour
- AI interaction behaviour
- Deployment behaviour
- Authentication behaviour
- Billing interaction behaviour
- Responsive interaction behaviour
- Keyboard interaction
- Accessibility interaction

No page may invent its own interaction rules when a rule already exists here.

======================================================================
2. CORE PRINCIPLE
======================================================================

ZyrionOS must feel like ONE operating system.

The user must always understand:

1. Where they are.
2. What they are doing.
3. What the system is doing.
4. Whether an action succeeded.
5. Whether an action failed.
6. What they can do next.
7. Whether their data was saved.
8. Whether an operation is still running.

The interface must never leave the user guessing.

======================================================================
3. GLOBAL INTERACTION MODEL
======================================================================

Every meaningful user action follows:

USER ACTION
    ↓
VALIDATION
    ↓
SYSTEM FEEDBACK
    ↓
API / LOCAL OPERATION
    ↓
SUCCESS / FAILURE
    ↓
UI STATE UPDATE
    ↓
NEXT AVAILABLE ACTION

Example:

Create Project
    ↓
Validate name
    ↓
Show processing state
    ↓
Backend request
    ↓
Project created
    ↓
Update project state
    ↓
Open Workspace

======================================================================
4. FULL-PAGE NAVIGATION RULE
======================================================================

Major application sections must occupy the complete application viewport.

Examples:

Create New Project
    → Full Workspace

Workspace
    → Full Workspace application

Settings
    → Full Settings page

Billing
    → Full Billing page

AI Chat
    → Full AI Chat experience

Projects
    → Full Projects page

Deployments
    → Full Deployment page

Analytics
    → Full Analytics page

Security
    → Full Security page

Profile
    → Full Profile page

A major page must NOT appear as a small card inside another page unless
the product specification explicitly defines it as a modal, drawer or
embedded panel.

======================================================================
5. NAVIGATION RULES
======================================================================

5.1 Primary navigation

Primary navigation changes the application section.

Example:

Dashboard
Projects
Workspace
AI
Deployments
Analytics
Billing
Settings

Each destination receives the complete available application area.

5.2 Active navigation

The current navigation item must have:

- Active visual state
- Clear contrast
- Persistent indication
- Accessible active state

5.3 Navigation during processing

If an important operation is running:

- Do not silently destroy the operation.
- Preserve operation state.
- Prevent accidental duplicate execution.
- Navigation may continue only when safe.
- Long-running operations must be recoverable.

======================================================================
6. CREATE NEW PROJECT FLOW
======================================================================

The Create New Project action is a major product action.

Flow:

Create New Project
        ↓
Project creation interface
        ↓
User enters project requirements
        ↓
User selects framework/configuration
        ↓
Validation
        ↓
Create request
        ↓
Project creation state
        ↓
Project successfully created
        ↓
Navigate to FULL WORKSPACE

The Workspace must become the primary screen.

The user must NOT remain inside a small creation card.

======================================================================
7. WORKSPACE INTERACTION MODEL
======================================================================

Workspace is an application environment, not a normal dashboard card.

Workspace must support:

- Project context
- AI command input
- Project files
- Code
- Preview
- Build status
- Deployment status
- Logs
- Project configuration
- Runtime information
- AI generation
- Project actions

Workspace interaction hierarchy:

APPLICATION HEADER
    ↓
PROJECT CONTEXT
    ↓
WORKSPACE TOOLBAR
    ↓
MAIN WORKSPACE CANVAS
    ↓
COMMAND / AI INPUT
    ↓
OPTIONAL INSPECTOR / PANEL

Workspace must maximize usable screen space.

======================================================================
8. BUTTON STATES
======================================================================

Every actionable button must support appropriate states.

Required states:

DEFAULT
HOVER
FOCUS
ACTIVE
DISABLED
LOADING
SUCCESS
ERROR

Not every button needs visible text for every state, but behaviour must
remain clear.

Example:

Build
    ↓
Building...
    ↓
Build complete

During processing:

- Prevent duplicate request.
- Prevent accidental double click.
- Preserve button dimensions where possible.
- Show meaningful processing feedback.

======================================================================
9. FORM INTERACTION
======================================================================

Every form must provide:

- Clear labels
- Correct input types
- Validation
- Error messages
- Processing state
- Success state
- Accessible focus behaviour

Validation must happen:

1. Before request.
2. On backend.
3. After backend response where required.

Frontend validation must never be considered security.

Backend remains authoritative.

======================================================================
10. INPUT BEHAVIOUR
======================================================================

Inputs must clearly communicate:

DEFAULT
FOCUS
FILLED
INVALID
DISABLED
READ-ONLY
LOADING

Errors must appear close to the affected field.

Do not rely exclusively on:

- Red borders
- Toast messages
- Icons

The user must understand what needs correction.

======================================================================
11. API REQUEST STATES
======================================================================

Every API-connected component must understand:

IDLE
LOADING
SUCCESS
EMPTY
ERROR
RETRY

Example:

IDLE
 ↓
LOADING
 ↓
SUCCESS

or

IDLE
 ↓
LOADING
 ↓
ERROR
 ↓
RETRY

No API-connected screen may remain visually ambiguous during a request.

======================================================================
12. LOADING EXPERIENCE
======================================================================

Loading must communicate actual system activity.

Use the appropriate loading mechanism:

Short operation:
    → Button loading

Data loading:
    → Skeleton

Page loading:
    → Page-level loading state

Large operation:
    → Progress / processing state

AI generation:
    → AI processing experience

Deployment:
    → Deployment progress experience

Never use a loading spinner everywhere by default.

======================================================================
13. EMPTY STATES
======================================================================

Empty states must be useful.

Every empty state should explain:

1. What is empty.
2. Why it may be empty when useful.
3. What the user can do next.

Example:

No Projects

You have not created a project yet.

[Create New Project]

Never display only:

"No data."

======================================================================
14. ERROR STATES
======================================================================

Errors must be understandable and actionable.

Error hierarchy:

FIELD ERROR
    ↓
COMPONENT ERROR
    ↓
REQUEST ERROR
    ↓
PAGE ERROR
    ↓
SYSTEM ERROR

Where possible provide:

- What happened
- What was affected
- What the user can do
- Retry action

Example:

"Project deployment failed."

[Retry Deployment]

Do not expose:

- Stack traces
- Internal server paths
- Secrets
- API keys
- Database information
- Internal infrastructure details

======================================================================
15. SUCCESS STATES
======================================================================

Successful operations must provide confirmation when useful.

Examples:

Project created
Project saved
Deployment completed
Settings updated
Payment completed
API key created

Feedback can use:

- Inline confirmation
- Toast
- Status badge
- Page state
- Navigation to next stage

Success feedback must never interrupt the workflow unnecessarily.

======================================================================
16. TOAST SYSTEM
======================================================================

Toasts are for short-lived informational feedback.

Appropriate:

- Saved successfully
- Copied
- Deployment started
- Settings updated

Not appropriate:

- Critical instructions
- Complex validation
- Important legal information
- Large error explanations
- Information the user must act on

Critical information belongs in the relevant interface.

======================================================================
17. MODAL RULES
======================================================================

Modals are reserved for focused decisions.

Use modals for:

- Confirmation
- Destructive actions
- Important configuration
- Short focused workflows

Do NOT use modals for:

- Full Settings
- Full Billing
- Full Workspace
- Large dashboards
- Complex application screens

Those must use full-page navigation.

======================================================================
18. DRAWER RULES
======================================================================

Drawers are secondary contextual interfaces.

Appropriate:

- Quick project details
- File information
- Activity details
- Secondary configuration
- Contextual inspector

A drawer must never become a hidden replacement for a major page.

======================================================================
19. DESTRUCTIVE ACTIONS
======================================================================

Destructive actions require explicit confirmation when the action is
irreversible or materially dangerous.

Examples:

Delete Project
Delete Account
Remove Integration
Cancel Subscription
Delete API Key

Confirmation must clearly state:

- What will be removed.
- Whether recovery is possible.
- What the user needs to confirm.

Avoid ambiguous buttons such as:

"Continue"

Prefer:

"Delete Project"

======================================================================
20. SEARCH INTERACTION
======================================================================

Global search must behave consistently throughout ZyrionOS.

Search should support:

- Input
- Loading
- Results
- No results
- Error
- Clear action

Search results must be grouped logically when multiple entity types exist.

Example:

Projects
Files
Deployments
Users
Settings
Documentation

Keyboard navigation should be supported where practical.

======================================================================
21. FILTERING & SORTING
======================================================================

Filters must clearly communicate:

- Current filter
- Number of active filters
- Clear filters
- Result count where appropriate

Sorting must clearly communicate:

- Current sort field
- Ascending/descending direction

Changing filters must never silently reset unrelated user selections.

======================================================================
22. PAGINATION
======================================================================

Large datasets must not load unnecessarily.

Pagination or controlled loading must preserve:

- Current filters
- Current sort
- Current page
- Search query

Changing page must provide appropriate loading feedback.

======================================================================
23. AUTO-SAVE
======================================================================

Auto-save may be used where appropriate.

When auto-save exists, show a subtle status:

Saving...
Saved
Save failed

Never make the user guess whether their work was saved.

======================================================================
24. UNSAVED CHANGES
======================================================================

If leaving a screen could cause meaningful data loss:

Show a confirmation.

Example:

Unsaved changes

You have changes that have not been saved.

[Stay]
[Leave]

Do not show unnecessary confirmations when no meaningful data can be lost.

======================================================================
25. AI CHAT INTERACTION
======================================================================

AI Chat is a first-class ZyrionOS interface.

It must support:

- User message
- AI response
- Processing state
- Streaming where supported
- Error state
- Retry
- Copy
- Regenerate where supported
- Conversation history
- Context awareness
- Attachments where supported
- Code output
- Tool/action status

AI must clearly distinguish:

USER
AI
SYSTEM / TOOL STATUS

The chat interface must not feel like a basic text box.

======================================================================
26. AI PROCESSING STATES
======================================================================

AI operations may have multiple stages.

Example:

Understanding request
    ↓
Planning
    ↓
Executing
    ↓
Generating
    ↓
Validating
    ↓
Completed

Only expose stages that are meaningful and accurate.

Never fake progress.

======================================================================
27. AI CODE GENERATION
======================================================================

Code generation flow:

User Request
    ↓
Validation
    ↓
AI Processing
    ↓
Code Generation
    ↓
Validation
    ↓
Project File Update
    ↓
Preview / Build
    ↓
Ready

Generated code must not be presented as successfully built merely because
the AI returned text.

Generation and successful build are separate states.

======================================================================
28. DEPLOYMENT INTERACTION
======================================================================

Deployment is a long-running operation.

Required conceptual states:

NOT DEPLOYED
QUEUED
BUILDING
DEPLOYING
DEPLOYED
FAILED

The UI must reflect the actual backend deployment state.

Never show "Deployed" simply because the deployment request was accepted.

======================================================================
29. DEPLOYMENT RETRY
======================================================================

When deployment fails:

Show:

- Failure status
- Useful reason when available
- Retry action
- Relevant logs when available

Retry must create a controlled deployment request.

Duplicate deployment requests must be prevented.

======================================================================
30. BILLING INTERACTION
======================================================================

Billing is a full application section.

Billing must support:

- Current plan
- Plan comparison
- Usage
- Payment status
- Invoices
- Subscription management
- Upgrade
- Downgrade where supported
- Cancellation where supported

Payment-related actions must always show clear processing and result states.

Never display payment success based only on frontend assumptions.

Backend/payment provider confirmation is authoritative.

======================================================================
31. SETTINGS INTERACTION
======================================================================

Settings is a full-page application area.

Settings should be organized by logical categories.

Examples:

Account
Profile
Security
Authentication
Notifications
Workspace
AI
Integrations
API
Billing
Preferences

Saving one category must not unintentionally reset another.

======================================================================
32. AUTHENTICATION INTERACTION
======================================================================

Authentication screens must provide:

- Login
- Signup
- Password recovery
- Verification where required
- OAuth where supported
- Loading
- Validation
- Error
- Success
- Session handling

Authentication errors must be understandable without exposing sensitive
backend information.

======================================================================
33. SESSION EXPIRATION
======================================================================

If the user's session expires:

1. Detect authentication failure.
2. Preserve useful local UI state where safe.
3. Explain that authentication is required.
4. Redirect to authentication when appropriate.
5. Avoid infinite redirect loops.

======================================================================
34. RESPONSIVE INTERACTION
======================================================================

Responsive behaviour must be intentional.

Desktop:
    Full enterprise workspace.

Tablet:
    Condensed navigation and panels.

Mobile:
    Prioritize the primary task.
    Secondary panels become navigational/contextual layers.

Do not simply shrink desktop UI.

Layouts must adapt structurally.

======================================================================
35. KEYBOARD INTERACTION
======================================================================

Important workflows should support keyboard interaction.

Examples:

Enter
    → Submit where appropriate

Shift + Enter
    → New line in AI command/chat input

Escape
    → Close modal/drawer where appropriate

Tab
    → Move through interactive controls

Arrow keys
    → Navigate supported menus/results

Keyboard shortcuts must never interfere with normal text entry.

======================================================================
36. FOCUS MANAGEMENT
======================================================================

When a modal opens:

→ Move focus appropriately.

When a modal closes:

→ Return focus to the triggering control where possible.

When navigation changes:

→ Focus should move logically to the new page content.

Focus must remain visible.

======================================================================
37. ACCESSIBILITY
======================================================================

Interactive components must have:

- Accessible names
- Keyboard access
- Focus states
- Correct semantic elements
- Appropriate ARIA only where required
- Sufficient contrast
- Error identification
- Status communication

Never use a clickable <div> where a button or link is appropriate.

======================================================================
38. DOUBLE-SUBMISSION PROTECTION
======================================================================

Actions that create, delete, deploy, purchase or otherwise mutate data
must prevent accidental duplicate execution.

During processing:

- Disable duplicate action.
- Preserve current request state.
- Re-enable after completion/failure.

======================================================================
39. DATA REFRESH RULES
======================================================================

After a successful mutation:

Update local UI state immediately where safe.

Then synchronize with backend data where necessary.

Example:

Create Project
    ↓
Update project list
    ↓
Select project
    ↓
Navigate Workspace
    ↓
Backend remains authoritative

Never require a full browser refresh for normal application operation.

======================================================================
40. STATE OWNERSHIP
======================================================================

Each state must have a clear owner.

SERVER STATE:
    Backend/API data

UI STATE:
    Tabs, modals, drawers, temporary selections

FORM STATE:
    User input

AUTH STATE:
    Session/user authentication

OPERATION STATE:
    Build, deployment, AI execution

Do not duplicate server state unnecessarily across unrelated components.

======================================================================
41. COMPONENT BEHAVIOUR
======================================================================

Reusable components must behave consistently.

Examples:

Button
Input
Select
Dropdown
Modal
Drawer
Toast
Tabs
Table
Card
Badge
Command Bar
File Explorer
Code Editor
AI Message
Status Indicator

A component must not change fundamental interaction behaviour merely
because it appears on a different page.

======================================================================
42. PAGE-LEVEL STATE MODEL
======================================================================

Every enterprise page should conceptually support:

INITIAL
LOADING
READY
EMPTY
ERROR
PROCESSING
SUCCESS

Not every page needs every state visibly.

The implementation must intentionally decide which states apply.

======================================================================
43. SECURITY INTERACTION PRINCIPLE
======================================================================

Frontend behaviour is NOT a security boundary.

The frontend may:

- Hide unavailable actions.
- Disable unavailable controls.
- Display permission states.

The backend must enforce:

- Authentication
- Authorization
- Ownership
- Validation
- Payment state
- Deployment permissions
- API permissions

Never trust frontend-only restrictions.

======================================================================
44. PERFORMANCE INTERACTION
======================================================================

The interface must remain responsive.

Use:

- Lazy loading where appropriate
- Pagination
- Debounced search where appropriate
- Controlled API requests
- Cached server state where appropriate
- Optimized rendering
- Code splitting

Do not sacrifice correctness for perceived speed.

======================================================================
45. NO FAKE UI PRINCIPLE
======================================================================

ZyrionOS must never pretend an operation happened when it did not.

Examples:

Do not show:
    "Deployment successful"

unless deployment actually succeeded.

Do not show:
    "Project generated"

unless project generation completed.

Do not show:
    "Saved"

unless the save operation succeeded.

Do not show:
    "Payment successful"

unless payment confirmation is authoritative.

Visual feedback must represent real system state.

======================================================================
46. INTERACTION CONSISTENCY RULE
======================================================================

The same action must behave the same way everywhere.

Example:

Delete

must use the same conceptual confirmation model.

Deploy

must use the same deployment state model.

Save

must use the same save feedback model.

Error

must use the same error communication model.

======================================================================
47. FINAL USER EXPERIENCE PRINCIPLE
======================================================================

The user should feel:

"I know where I am."

"I know what I can do."

"I know what ZyrionOS is doing."

"I know whether it worked."

"I know what happens next."

This is the core interaction standard for the entire platform.

======================================================================
48. IMPLEMENTATION RULE
======================================================================

Before implementing any frontend page or component:

1. Check Document 01 for product/UX intent.
2. Check Document 02 for frontend placement.
3. Check Document 03 for visual design system.
4. Check Document 04 for component/page architecture.
5. Check Document 05 for data/API/system integration rules.
6. Apply Document 06 for interaction and state behaviour.

If a proposed implementation conflicts with these documents:

STOP.

Do not create a local workaround.

Resolve the architectural conflict before implementation.

